{{smaller|'''Empire of the West.''' See ''Western Empire'', under ''empire.''}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Situated in, on, or to the west; being or lying westward with reference to something else; western: as, the ''West'' Indies; ''West'' Virginia; the ''west'' bank or the ''west'' fork of a river; ''west'' longitude.
{{smaller block|This shall be your ''west'' border. {{right|Num. xxxiv. 6.}}
Go thou with her to the ''west'' end of the wood.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., v. 3. 9.}}}}
'''2.''' Coming or moving from the west or western region: as, a ''west'' wind.—'''3.''' ''Eccles.'', situated in, or in the direction of, that part of a church which is furthest from the altar or high altar; opposite the ecclesiastical east.—{{smaller|'''West dial.''' See ''dial.''—'''West End,''' the western part of London; specifically, the fashionable or aristocratic quarter: often used attributively.}}

'''west''' (west), ''adv.'' [See ''west'', ''n.''] To or toward the west; westward or westerly; specifically (''eccles.''), toward or in the direction of that part of a church which is furthest from the altar or high altar.
{{smaller block|Go ''west'', young man, and grow up with the country.
{{right|''Horace Greeley.''}}}}

'''west''' (west), ''v. i.'' [< ME. ''westen''; < ''west'', ''n.''] To move toward the west; turn or veer to the west. [Rare or obsolete.]
{{smaller block|<poem>On a bed of gold she lay to reste
Tyl that the hote sonne gan to ''weste.''</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Parliament of Fowls, l. 266.}}
<poem>Twice hath he risen where he now doth ''West'',
And ''wested'' twice where he ought rise aright.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., V., Prol., st. 8.}}}}

